**commonly used Ansible modules**

These modules help you manage system-related tasks like users, groups, files, and packages.
•	user: Manages user accounts.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.user
o	Use case: Create, delete, or modify users.
•	group: Manages groups on the target system.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.group
o	Use case: Create, delete, or modify user groups.
•	file: Manages files and directories (permissions, ownership, etc.).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.file
o	Use case: Set file permissions, change ownership, or create directories.
•	service: Manages services (start, stop, restart, etc.).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.service
o	Use case: Start, stop, restart, or enable/disable services.
•	package: Installs or removes software packages (use apt, yum, etc., as specific backends).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.package
o	Use case: Install or remove packages from the system.
•	systemd: Manages systemd services and units.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.systemd
o	Use case: Manage services for systems using systemd as the init system.
2. Network Modules
These modules manage network-related tasks, including configuring network devices or servers.
•	ping: Checks whether a target is reachable.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.ping
o	Use case: Simple connectivity check.
•	firewalld: Manages firewalld (firewall management for Linux).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.firewalld
o	Use case: Add, remove, or list firewalld rules.
•	ufw: Manages the Uncomplicated Firewall (UFW).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.ufw
o	Use case: Enable/disable UFW or add/remove rules.
3. Package Management Modules
Used to install, remove, and manage packages on various distributions.
•	apt: Manages packages using apt on Debian-based systems (Ubuntu, etc.).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.apt
o	Use case: Install, update, or remove packages.
•	yum: Manages packages using yum on RHEL/CentOS systems.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.yum
o	Use case: Install, update, or remove packages.
•	pip: Manages Python packages using pip.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.pip
o	Use case: Install or uninstall Python packages.
•	dnf: Manages packages using dnf on newer RHEL/CentOS versions (8+).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.dnf
o	Use case: Install, update, or remove packages using dnf.
4. File and Template Management
These modules allow you to manage files, templates, and content on target hosts.
•	copy: Copies files to remote hosts.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.copy
o	Use case: Copy a file from local to remote machines.
•	template: Jinja2 templates files and copies them to remote hosts.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.template
o	Use case: Dynamically generate files (e.g., configuration files).
•	fetch: Fetches files from remote hosts to the local machine.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.fetch
o	Use case: Download files from remote hosts to the local machine.
•	lineinfile: Ensures a specific line is in a file or removes it.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.lineinfile
o	Use case: Add or modify specific lines in configuration files.
•	blockinfile: Adds or removes a block of text in a file.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.blockinfile
o	Use case: Manage large blocks of content inside files.
5. Database Modules
These modules are useful for interacting with databases.
•	mysql_db: Manages MySQL databases.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.mysql_db
o	Use case: Create or delete databases, execute SQL queries.
•	postgresql_db: Manages PostgreSQL databases.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.postgresql_db
o	Use case: Create, delete, or manage PostgreSQL databases.
•	mongodb: Manages MongoDB databases.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.mongodb
o	Use case: Manage MongoDB databases, users, and roles.
6. Cloud Modules
These modules are used to interact with cloud services like AWS, GCP, and Azure.
•	ec2: Manages EC2 instances in AWS.
o	Example: community.aws.ec2
o	Use case: Launch, terminate, and manage EC2 instances.
•	gcp_compute_instance: Manages Google Cloud Compute instances.
o	Example: google.cloud.gcp_compute_instance
o	Use case: Create, delete, and manage Google Cloud VMs.
•	azure_rm_virtualmachine: Manages Azure virtual machines.
o	Example: azure.azcollection.azure_rm_virtualmachine
o	Use case: Create, delete, and manage Azure VMs.
7. Container Modules
For managing containers, Docker, and Kubernetes.
•	docker_container: Manages Docker containers.
o	Example: community.docker.docker_container
o	Use case: Start, stop, and manage Docker containers.
•	k8s: Manages Kubernetes resources.
o	Example: community.kubernetes.k8s
o	Use case: Manage Kubernetes resources like deployments, services, and pods.
8. Monitoring and Metrics Modules
These modules help you monitor and gather metrics from your systems.
•	cron: Manages cron jobs.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.cron
o	Use case: Add, remove, or manage cron jobs.
•	slurp: Slurps a file's content into base64.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.slurp
o	Use case: Get content from a file and process it in Ansible.
•	gather_facts: Collects system facts (e.g., IP address, OS version, etc.).
o	Example: ansible.builtin.gather_facts
o	Use case: Collect system information before proceeding with the playbook.
9. Security and Encryption Modules
Manage encryption, authentication, and permissions.
•	authorized_key: Manages SSH authorized keys for users.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.authorized_key
o	Use case: Add or remove SSH keys for users.
•	seboolean: Manages SELinux booleans.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.seboolean
o	Use case: Set or unset SELinux booleans for security.
10. Other Utility Modules
These modules provide various utilities.
•	debug: Displays messages during playbook execution.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.debug
o	Use case: Print variables or debug output for debugging.
•	wait_for: Waits for a condition to be true, useful for waiting for servers or ports.
o	Example: ansible.builtin.wait_for
o	Use case: Wait for a port or server to become available.

